(>> 1.6 University Review & Examination Questions) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Q1: Differentiate comprehensively between GET and POST HTTP request methods in) Tj
T*
(terms of data transmission, caching, idempotency, and security.) Tj
T*
(Answer: GET requests request data from a specified resource; form parameters are) Tj
T*
(appended directly to the URL string as key-value pairs \(query parameters\). GET requests) Tj
T*
(are idempotent \(repeating identical requests causes no server state change\), cached by) Tj
T*
(browsers, bookmarked, and visible in browser history and server logs. Consequently, GET) Tj
T*
(must NEVER transmit sensitive data \(passwords, credit cards\). POST requests submit data) Tj
T*
(to be processed by a resource; data is transmitted within the HTTP request body. POST is) Tj
T*
(non-idempotent \(resubmitting creates duplicate records\), never cached by default, not) Tj
T*
(saved in history, and has no data length restrictions, making it mandatory for secure) Tj
T*
(form submissions and database mutations.) Tj
T*
(* Q2: Explain the significance of Semantic HTML5 elements over non-semantic <div>) Tj
T*
(tags, highlighting impacts on Accessibility \(A11y\) and SEO.) Tj
T*
(Answer: Non-semantic tags like <div> and <span> tell the browser nothing about their) Tj
T*
(contents, requiring arbitrary class names \(e.g. <div class='header'>\) that differ) Tj
T*
(between developers. Semantic elements \(<header>, <nav>, <main>, <article>, <section>,) Tj
T*
(<aside>, <footer>\) carry explicit structural and functional meaning. For Accessibility,) Tj
T*
(screen readers use semantic tags to provide landmark navigation shortcuts, allowing) Tj
T*
(visually impaired users to jump directly to main content or skip navigation menus. For) Tj
T*
(Search Engine Optimization \(SEO\), web crawlers parse semantic tags to determine) Tj
T*
(information hierarchy and prioritize core article text over secondary sidebar) Tj
T*
(advertisements.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 1.7 Key Takeaways and Revision Points) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* HTTP is a stateless client-server request-response protocol running over TCP.) Tj
T*
(* Semantic HTML5 tags \(<header>, <nav>, <main>, <footer>\) improve accessibility and) Tj
T*
(SEO.) Tj
T*
(* HTML5 form validation attributes \(required, pattern, min/max\) validate input) Tj
T*
(without JS.) Tj
T*
(* The DOM represents the parsed document as an in-memory hierarchical object tree.) Tj
T*
ET

